okay so in this short video we're gonna  compare and contrast convergent  evolution to divergent evolution so  let's go ahead and get started well  let's start by focusing on divergent  evolution the evolution of two or more  species from a common ancestor you know  kind of the classic example that you're  often gonna run across it is the Darwin  finches you may know that Charles Darwin  set upon a journey where he explored the  Galapagos Islands and on the Galapagos  Islands were many species of finches and  so the Darwin Finch is a great example  of divergent evolution how did there  become so many species of these finches  well it is believed that if we look at  the the map here and on mainland South  America lived the ancestral species of  today's Darwin finches and over time  some happened to migrate over towards  the Galapagos Islands the small grouping  of islands off the western coast of  Ecuador if we zoom on in when we zoom on  in here we see the the the original  finches that migrated here from the  coast of South America and as time  passed finches would multiply they would  reproduce with one another and multiply  and they would adapt to their  surroundings so they multiply but then  some of them of course might venture out  and explore and migrate to other islands  which have slightly different conditions  than the island they just came from so  they over time begin to adapt to these  this new island and multiply and as time  goes by they begin to again migrate  outward and they come to a third island  which has slightly different conditions  than the other islands and as time  passes they reproduce and multiply and  they adapt to the conditions of this  island and then as time passes some  begin to migrate outward and they come  to yet another island on the Galapagos  that has slightly different features  than all the others  so they adapt they multiply that type  they diversify and eventually the  process continues where again some may  migrate to another yet another island  with its own unique environment and they  begin to adapt and multiply and and  basically take on the characteristics  best able to survive in this particular  environment and today there's actually  13 different species of Darwin finches  but you can see why it's called  divergent evolution I hope these 13 have  diverged from the one common ancestor  that used to live on a mainland of South  America so here's a kind of another  example here of divergent evolution  closer to home here in for those of us  who are in the United States watching  this video you know the Grand Canyon  today is just this magnificent  geographic feature in the American  Southwest well but it wasn't always that  way  so there are these squirrels that live  in the Grand Canyon's and you know right  now let's go back in time and right now  we can see the squirrels I have gene  flow they're able to migrate between the  northern and southern areas of this of  this ecosystem here but as time passes  and the Grand Canyon forms so as time  passes and the Grand Canyon becomes what  it is today  notice how the two populations of  squirrels are no longer able to  reproduce with one another there's no  gene flow the two groups have become  isolated from one another and this has  led to the divergence of the ancestral  species today there are the Kaibab  squirrels of the northern rim and the a  brute squirrels of the southern rim but  because they've diverged from one common  ancestor I think it's a really nice  visual example of divergent evolution  and so really the pattern that we see is  that species will adapt to the  environment where they live through the  acts of natural selection organisms that  are best suited for let let's say the  Noir  rym are more likely to survive and  reproduce but that's probably gonna be a  little different than the organisms best  suited for the southern rim who are  gonna survive and reproduce a little  differently okay then so what is  convergent evolution well this is when  unrelated species will independently  evolved similar traits well why why  would unrelated species independently  evolved similar traits well we noticed  that is that when we see examples of  this they tend to share a similar  environment or a similar ecological  niche you know a real nice visual  example of this comes in in ocean  organisms like the tuna example tuna is  a type of fish and dolphins which  although look very fish like in their  appearance and dolphins I'm sure you may  know are mammals and yet and then  there's also penguins penguins birds and  yet they too have some very fish-like  features to them so notice how the  bodies of these animals very different  organisms here the tuna the mammal the  tuna which is a fish that dolphin which  is a man when the penguin which is a  bird unrelated species to one another  and yet they've evolved similar  characteristics well what do they have  in common well they they live in the  similar a similar environment they live  in the oceans and whether you're a fish  or a mammal or a bird if you live and  survive in the ocean then there are  certain traits that will lead your  survival having a smooth body and  streamlined body with fins and flippers  to help propel yourself through the  water these are advantages that you're  gonna pass along to your offspring over  time and whether you're a fish whether  you're even ma'am or whether you're a  bird these are traits that become more  common over time another example would  be in this example here flying squirrels  which actually are rodents and sugar  gliders which are marsupials marsupials  you may know are related to animals like  kangaroos so flying squirrels being  rodents sugar gliders being marsupials  are not really related at all and yet  when you examine them they look very  similar to one another to have these  skin flaps in between the front legs and  their hind legs which allows them to  kind of glide short distances as they're  jumping through trees so they share a  common environment you know being that  they're tree living organisms and so  they've adapted to that similar  environment they've adapted these common  traits and so really the pattern we  learn here is that if different species  live in similar environments that over  time they'll converge they'll become  they will develop common features well  let's kind of wrap this up with a little  conclusion here again divergent  evolution the evolution of two or more  species from a common ancestor you know  this drawing right here I totally  believe it's pronounced yo hippest is  believed to be the common ancestor of  organisms like donkeys and zebras and  horses but again a great example of  divergence evolution because donkeys and  horses and zebras have diverged to  become different species originating  from this one common ancestor here and  yet we can contrast that with convergent  evolution which again is the is when  unrelated species independently evolved  similar traits kind of another  interesting example of convergent  evolution is when we look at eagles  which is a type of bird and bats which  are a type of mammal and you know a  butterfly which is a type of insect  these are very unrelated species again  birds and mammals and insects they're  not related and yet they've converged on  their ability to fly they've taken  advantage of the common environment  where they live and that would be like  the air so again I hope you found this  video helpful too short a short video to  compare and contrast divergent versus  convergent evolution thanks for watching
 

